problems.problem:
The number of my hotel room is a three-digit integer. I thought that the same number could be obtained by multiplying together all of:
(i) one more than the first digit;
(ii) one more than the second digit;
(iii) the third digit.
Prove that I was mistaken.

How many distinct triangles satisfy all the following properties:
(i) all three side-lengths are a whole number of centimetres in length;
(ii) at least one side is of length $10$ cm;
(iii) at least one side-length is the (arithmetic) mean of the other two side-lengths?
